  "summary": "Intertextuality is the web of echoes, allusions, quotations, and patterns that link biblical texts across the canon.",
  "historical_context": "Biblical authors wrote within a tradition of remembering, reusing, and reinterpreting earlier Scripture for new settings and audiences.",
  "ancient_near_east_context": "In the ancient world, authoritative texts were often reactivated through citation and echo, and the Bible uses that practice to show ongoing divine speech.",
  "literary_context": "Intertextuality is especially visible when the New Testament reads the Old Testament through Jesus, the church, and the end of the age.",
  "covenantal_significance": "Intertextual reading reveals that one covenantal story is being developed across many books, voices, and genres.",
